# Rank 1706 / CVE-2021-25900 (GHSA-43w2-9j62-hq99, CWE-787):
# smallvec 0.6's bulk insert planned its allocation from an iterator's size
# hint. `Iterator::size_hint` returns a pair whose first element is only a
# *lower* bound on how many elements the iterator will yield -- the standard
# library documentation is explicit that an iterator may return more than its
# hint -- so `reserve(lower_size_bound)` is sound only as long as the code stops
# writing once it reaches that bound. insert_many did not:
#
#   let (lower_size_bound, _) = iter.size_hint();
#   self.reserve(lower_size_bound);
#   unsafe {
#       let mut ptr = self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index as isize);
#       ptr::copy(ptr, ptr.offset(lower_size_bound as isize), old_len - index);
#       self.set_len(index);                       // logical length truncated
#       let mut num_added = 0;
#       for element in iter {                      // driven by the iterator
#           let mut cur = ptr.offset(num_added as isize);
#           if num_added >= lower_size_bound {     // knows it passed the reserve
#               self.reserve(1);                   // grows by one element
#               ptr = self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index as isize);
#               cur = ptr.offset(num_added as isize);
#               ptr::copy(cur, cur.offset(1), old_len - index);
#           }
#           ptr::write(cur, element);
#           num_added += 1;
#       }
#       self.set_len(old_len + num_added);
#   }
#
# `reserve(additional)` sizes the allocation from the collection's *current
# length*, and `set_len(index)` has just cut that length to `index` while
# `ptr::copy` has placed the tail at `lower_size_bound` past it. The
# in-loop `reserve(1)` therefore guarantees capacity for `index + 1` elements
# while the write cursor is at `index + num_added`, so from the second element
# past the bound onward `ptr::write` lands outside the allocation. The advisory's
# own reproducer needs only `size_hint().0 == 0`, which any filter iterator
# gives, so nothing is reserved at all before the first write.
#
# 5757ac500d4e544485d796b542e4e589749c291b (backport of #254 to the 0.6 branch,
# fixes #253) removes the re-reserve entirely: the unsafe block writes at most
# `lower_size_bound` elements --
#
#   while num_added < lower_size_bound {
#       let element = match iter.next() { Some(x) => x, None => break };
#       let cur = ptr.offset(num_added as isize);
#       ptr::write(cur, element);
#       num_added += 1;
#   }
#
# -- and any surplus is inserted afterwards through the safe per-element
# `self.insert(index + num_added, element)`, which reserves per element from an
# accurate length. The pool row is correct: repository, language, CWE and commit
# all check, the vulnerable construct is present at 7852204 and the remediation
# at 5757ac5. No corpus correction.
#
# Vocabulary is checked for reuse before anything is written.
# code.FixedChunkCapacityGrowthIndex (VYQL-MEM-042, CWE-119/787, "capacity grows
# by a fixed chunk even though the requested index or level may jump beyond the
# new capacity") is the right weakness class and is bound today only in C; the
# candidate bindings below all emit it. code.CompressedBlockSizeCapacityMismatch
# is deflate-specific; code.LengthDerivedAllocation is an allocation sized
# before a bound is known, not a reservation grown by a constant while a write
# cursor advances; code.PointerOffsetBoundsCheck and code.RawMemoryCopySize are
# copy-length shapes. No concept or rule ID is minted.
#
# What fires today. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` over
# the vulnerable checkout reports 21 findings, every one VYQL-MEM-006
# (code.NullableDeref) on raw dereferences elsewhere in lib.rs -- 141, 327-646,
# 749-751, 796-816. Not one lands at or after lib.rs:820: insert_many (825-867)
# carries no finding of any kind, and smallvec's own correct single-element
# `insert` (809) and `push` (624) are the nearest labelled code. Over the fixed
# checkout the same scan reports 21 MEM-006 plus one VYQL-CFG-014 on the
# regression test the fixed revision adds; the delta is noise, not coverage.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap: the defect is a three-way correlation between the
# amount reserved (the size-hint lower bound), the write cursor (`num_added`)
# and the collection's logical length (truncated by `set_len`), and no fact the
# Rust frontend emits can state any relation among them. Three candidate
# presence bindings are built and measured against both revisions of the real
# lib.rs and against the standard library's own `Vec::extend_desugared`
# (library/alloc/src/vec/mod.rs), which is the canonical *correct* form of this
# exact pattern and the false positive any of these shapes buys:
#
#   while let Some(element) = iterator.next() {
#       let len = self.len();
#       if len == self.capacity() {
#           let (lower, _) = iterator.size_hint();
#           self.reserve(lower.saturating_add(1));
#       }
#       unsafe { ptr::write(self.as_mut_ptr().add(len), element);
#                self.set_len(len + 1); }
#   }
#
# 1. `size_hint` + `ptr::write` + `reserve` + `set_len` -- the four APIs of the
#    defect -- is also the exact API set of extend_desugared. Discriminates
#    nothing: std has all four, in the correct order, and is the most-read Rust
#    file in existence.
# 2. Narrowing the reserve to a literal, `containsAny(node.context.callArg,
#    ["reserve:1"])`, does discriminate on the CVE pair (1 finding at lib.rs:825,
#    0 on the fixed revision) and stays at 0 over 24 other Rust-bearing trees
#    under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/, but reports 1 HIGH VYQL-MEM-042 on
#    library/alloc/src/vec/mod.rs:2488 -- identically on the vulnerable, fixed,
#    altfix, allocv and allocf revisions of that tree (and on the copy under
#    rank1699), because `callArgContextTokensScoped` flow-expands a call
#    argument through its inputs (internal/bindings/bindings_match.go:1304) and
#    so reads `self.reserve(lower.saturating_add(1))` as containing the literal
#    `1`. One true positive bought at the price of a HIGH-severity buffer
#    overflow row on the reference implementation of the safe form, on every
#    revision of it. Unshippable.
# 3. `containsAny(node.context.assign, ["=self.as_mut_ptr"])` -- the raw pointer
#    re-derived from the collection's base -- is the only token that separates
#    all three (present in the vulnerable insert_many, absent from the patched
#    one and from extend_desugared). It is a spelling artifact, not the defect:
#    extend_desugared escapes only because it reads the base pointer inline
#    (`self.as_mut_ptr().add(len)`) rather than through a reassigned variable,
#    and the vulnerable loop written the inline way -- `ptr::write(
#    self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index + num_added), element)` -- would keep the
#    out-of-bounds write and drop the token. A binding whose discriminator
#    vanishes under a reskin of the same bug is the fixing commit's own layout
#    in disguise.
#
# Two further facts close the data route. The Rust frontend emits no loop or
# ordering tokens at all -- the structured-context walk in
# internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/rust.go (~line 600) handles only
# assignment_expression, call_expression, field_expression and match_arm, so
# `for`/`while` leave no trace and nothing says a `reserve` ran after a
# `set_len`. And the clearing check the concept's review twin names,
# core.MemoryBoundsCheck, has exactly one binding in the tree and it is Go
# (vyql/bindings/go/m48a985c1.vyql:1593), so a Rust emission of
# code.FixedChunkCapacityGrowthIndex would be unsuppressible by its own
# documented control.
#
# Behaviour a shippable binding needs: a Rust function-context fact that carries
# the loop structure of the body -- specifically whether a raw write sits inside
# a loop, and whether a reserve or a set_len precedes it within that loop -- or,
# equivalently, a correlation between a reserved count and a write index. This
# is the Rust instance of the capacity/write-length family recorded in C
# at rank 1370 ("an output buffer's capacity parameter never correlated with a
# write length") and of the loop-trip-count family at 1297/1300/1305.
#
# The four cases below are the minimal pair, all rejected today. They differ only
# in what bounds the raw write: the iterator (vulnerable), the reserved count
# with a safe fallback (the fixed revision), the accurate length (std's correct form, the
# false positive any token shape buys) and a single reserved element (correct
# one-in-one-out, the false positive the bare reserve(1) pairing buys).
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, all four forms rejected.

test "rank1706: insert_many writes through a raw pointer past a size-hint reservation, growing capacity one element at a time"
  lang rust
  file lib.rs
  reject_review code.FixedChunkCapacityGrowthIndex
  code
  ```
  use std::ptr;

  pub struct SmallVec<T> { data: *mut T, cap: usize, len: usize }

  impl<T> SmallVec<T> {
      pub fn len(&self) -> usize { self.len }
      pub fn as_mut_ptr(&mut self) -> *mut T { self.data }
      pub fn set_len(&mut self, n: usize) { self.len = n }
      pub fn reserve(&mut self, additional: usize) { let _ = additional; }

      pub fn insert_many<I: IntoIterator<Item=T>>(&mut self, index: usize, iterable: I) {
          let iter = iterable.into_iter();
          if index == self.len() {
              return self.extend(iter);
          }

          let (lower_size_bound, _) = iter.size_hint();
          self.reserve(lower_size_bound);

          unsafe {
              let old_len = self.len();
              let mut ptr = self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index as isize);
              ptr::copy(ptr, ptr.offset(lower_size_bound as isize), old_len - index);
              self.set_len(index);

              let mut num_added = 0;
              for element in iter {
                  let mut cur = ptr.offset(num_added as isize);
                  if num_added >= lower_size_bound {
                      self.reserve(1);
                      ptr = self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index as isize);
                      cur = ptr.offset(num_added as isize);
                      ptr::copy(cur, cur.offset(1), old_len - index);
                  }
                  ptr::write(cur, element);
                  num_added += 1;
              }
              self.set_len(old_len + num_added);
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1706: the unsafe write loop bounded by the reserved count, surplus through the safe per-element insert"
  lang rust
  file lib.rs
  reject_review code.FixedChunkCapacityGrowthIndex
  code
  ```
  use std::ptr;

  pub struct SmallVec<T> { data: *mut T, cap: usize, len: usize }

  impl<T> SmallVec<T> {
      pub fn len(&self) -> usize { self.len }
      pub fn as_mut_ptr(&mut self) -> *mut T { self.data }
      pub fn set_len(&mut self, n: usize) { self.len = n }
      pub fn reserve(&mut self, additional: usize) { let _ = additional; }
      pub fn insert(&mut self, index: usize, element: T) { let _ = (index, element); }

      pub fn insert_many<I: IntoIterator<Item=T>>(&mut self, index: usize, iterable: I) {
          let mut iter = iterable.into_iter();
          if index == self.len() {
              return self.extend(iter);
          }

          let (lower_size_bound, _) = iter.size_hint();
          self.reserve(lower_size_bound);
          let mut num_added = 0;

          unsafe {
              let old_len = self.len();
              let ptr = self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index as isize);
              ptr::copy(ptr, ptr.offset(lower_size_bound as isize), old_len - index);
              self.set_len(index);

              while num_added < lower_size_bound {
                  let element = match iter.next() {
                      Some(x) => x,
                      None => break,
                  };
                  let cur = ptr.offset(num_added as isize);
                  ptr::write(cur, element);
                  num_added += 1;
              }
              self.set_len(old_len + num_added);
          }

          for element in iter {
              self.insert(index + num_added, element);
              num_added += 1;
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1706: a grow-when-full loop that reserves from an accurate length writes raw safely"
  lang rust
  file lib.rs
  reject_review code.FixedChunkCapacityGrowthIndex
  code
  ```
  use std::ptr;

  pub struct SmallVec<T> { data: *mut T, cap: usize, len: usize }

  impl<T> SmallVec<T> {
      pub fn len(&self) -> usize { self.len }
      pub fn capacity(&self) -> usize { self.cap }
      pub fn as_mut_ptr(&mut self) -> *mut T { self.data }
      pub fn set_len(&mut self, n: usize) { self.len = n }
      pub fn reserve(&mut self, additional: usize) { let _ = additional; }

      pub fn extend_desugared<I: Iterator<Item=T>>(&mut self, mut iterator: I) {
          while let Some(element) = iterator.next() {
              let len = self.len();
              if len == self.capacity() {
                  let (lower, _) = iterator.size_hint();
                  self.reserve(lower.saturating_add(1));
              }
              unsafe {
                  ptr::write(self.as_mut_ptr().add(len), element);
                  self.set_len(len + 1);
              }
          }
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1706: a single-element insert that reserves one and writes one"
  lang rust
  file lib.rs
  reject_review code.FixedChunkCapacityGrowthIndex
  code
  ```
  use std::ptr;

  pub struct SmallVec<T> { data: *mut T, cap: usize, len: usize }

  impl<T> SmallVec<T> {
      pub fn as_mut_ptr(&mut self) -> *mut T { self.data }
      pub fn reserve(&mut self, additional: usize) { let _ = additional; }
      pub fn set_len(&mut self, n: usize) { self.len = n }

      pub fn insert(&mut self, index: usize, element: T) {
          self.reserve(1);

          unsafe {
              let ptr = self.as_mut_ptr().offset(index as isize);
              ptr::copy(ptr, ptr.offset(1), 0);
              ptr::write(ptr, element);
              self.set_len(index + 1);
          }
      }
  }
  ```
